{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly asks to distinguish idempotent from safe operations 'drawing on published definitions' and permits proposing directed research fetches (allowlist: docs.python.org). SRC_001 instead grounds its distinction in 'observable behavior' and 'empirical observation' of production systems (WALs, Kafka, event sourcing), constructing its own definitions rather than citing or fetching published ones. It never proposes any research fetch despite the problem's explicit invitation. The core distinction (fixed-point vs. invariant-preservation) is reasonable but is presented as a personal formulation, not as drawn from published definitions, which is the standard the problem sets.","target":"35d54235dcfcee6838d7ede2c0edf3568e0fad1be8bdd5acf3ff87fcead7f908"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's opening claim that 'Idempotence at the consumer/processing layer implies safety at the storage/log layer' is a non sequitur. Consumer-side idempotence (reprocessing the same event yields no additional state change) and storage-side safety (appending a duplicate does not violate structural invariants) are independent properties: you can have idempotent consumers over a corrupted log, or safe append-only storage with non-idempotent consumers. The log's structural safety is an intrinsic consequence of append-only semantics, not an implication of reader idempotence. This logical gap weakens the two-boundary model's foundation, since the claimed implication relationship between the boundaries does not hold.","target":"fe91ea9f2df4d0678d6bb472a0cbb4efb03d324dfabaa8010614ec783870b8c9"}]}